rtues of 26 different  kinds of water  from rain  to water fountain pig troughs which  would heal snake bites if applied to the  wound  they also added fruit juices to flavor  the various waters  from the 6th century  tea was a popular drink at the tables of  the wealthy  of course the chinese also enjoyed  alcoholic beverages which were offered  at the table  while they had imported grapes for wine  that remained a rare treat  the real drink of choice for the men  from the tang dynasty until today was  rice wine  this was fermented grains  millet in the north and rice in the  south and it took skilled brewers to  make this  these rice wines were often flavored  with things like black pepper or fruit  juices  after a satisfying hours-long feast  accompanied by music  the emperor or other wealthy host  retired to his own quarters in the  compound  the men seldom stayed with their wives  the women were brought to his bedroom  for sexual intercourse  the man slept among his cushions and  furs surrounded by his servants who  slept on the floor of his room  this sample of medieval daily life can  show us the ways in which humans are  alike and how they're different  as we study the material life of the  past  we can see the things we use from the  houses that give us shelter to the food  that sustains us the clothes that  protect us and the items that amuse us  in every society we need all these  things and the differences that emerge  arise in large part  from the goods that were locally  accessible  vikings built with sod because they had  no wood  and societies that had a shortage of  wood  didn't use fireplaces  people who kept sheep wore wool  and those who had silk worms wore silk  the same principle applies to food  all over the world people seek out what  constitutes a balanced diet  we all want grains or some other  starches fruits and vegetables and  protein  societies like china that have a lot of  people and not much protein must eat  everything  locations like europe that actually had  more domestic animals than people  could afford to be choosy  beyond the basics another overriding  reality of humans  is the desire for fermented beverages  these are practical because they form a  way to preserve foods grains or grapes  but our desire goes beyond that  we like beverages  and other plants from tobacco to cava to  marijuana all available in the middle  ages that change our moods  the history of human diet tells us a lot  about our culture we are indeed  what we eat  finally this brief survey  shows us that humans are desperately  greedy for innovation  in our food we long for novelty which  established a valuable trade in things  like pepper or spices to change our  flavors and this is the same impulse  that will send christopher columbus  looking for the spice islands  in clothing the silk trade also brought  huge wealth  we don't need jewels artwork and other  items of beauty but we sure want them  and as we have seen throughout this  course this desire led to global trade  and great wealth  that was true in the middle ages and  it's true today

